{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NooAk5rAvVNDB1cPAGobEw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CODATA silicon lattice parameter a 5.431020511 angstrom NIST\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Lattice constant\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_constant\"},{\"title\":\"Lattice constant\",\"url\":\"https://Www.Wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_spacing\"},{\"title\":\"Template:Physical constants\",\"url\":\"https://wiki.riteme.site/wiki/Template:Physconst\"},{\"title\":\"Loading AI tools\",\"url\":\"https://wikiwand.com/en/Lattice_constants\"},{\"title\":\"Lattice spacing\",\"url\":\"https://demo.azizisearch.com/lite/wikipedia/page/Lattice_spacing\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2018\",\"url\":\"https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2018\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"www.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/node/691991\"},{\"title\":\"www.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/node/697111\"},{\"title\":\"www.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/node/731856\"}]\n\nThe search confirms the value. A NIST X-ray reference table from the 2018 CODATA adjustment lists the silicon lattice parameter as 5.431 020 511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, measured in vacuum at 22.5 °C, with a relative uncertainty of 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ lattice parameter∗ of Si (in vacuum, 22.5 ◦C) a 5.431 020 511(89)× 10−10 m 1.6× 10−8. The same NIST page notes that this is the lattice parameter (unit cell edge length) of an ideal single crystal of naturally occurring Si free of impurities and imperfections.\n\nA Wikipedia template that mirrors CODATA says its values were updated to the CODATA 2022 values and lists the same silicon value, 5.431020511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with a relative standard uncertainty of 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ lattice parameter of silicon | a = 5.431020511(89)×10−10 m | ur(a) = 1.6×10−8. So the value appears to be unchanged between the 2018 and 2022 adjustments, though I only confirmed the 2022 figure through a secondary source.\n\nFor background, NIST's published silicon lattice comparison papers, Kessler et al. (1994) and Hanke & Kessler (2005), appeared in the results, but I only saw their bibliographic records, not their contents.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"da0dc3b6-c5de-4e9f-8234-2ef78e8ae4a4","uuid":"12b32cd5-2d09-44b5-bce2-2e7f6459f46b","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:27:45.457Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CODATA silicon lattice parameter a 5.431020511 angstrom NIST","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01TGHdahmd5aZhf63bagH5tm","content":[{"title":"Lattice constant","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_constant"},{"title":"Lattice constant","url":"https://Www.Wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_spacing"},{"title":"Template:Physical constants","url":"https://wiki.riteme.site/wiki/Template:Physconst"},{"title":"Loading AI tools","url":"https://wikiwand.com/en/Lattice_constants"},{"title":"Lattice spacing","url":"https://demo.azizisearch.com/lite/wikipedia/page/Lattice_spacing"},{"title":"xray 2018","url":"https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf"},{"title":"xray 2018","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf"},{"title":"www.nist.gov","url":"https://www.nist.gov/node/691991"},{"title":"www.nist.gov","url":"https://www.nist.gov/node/697111"},{"title":"www.nist.gov","url":"https://www.nist.gov/node/731856"}]},"The search confirms the value. A NIST X-ray reference table from the 2018 CODATA adjustment lists the silicon lattice parameter as 5.431 020 511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, measured in vacuum at 22.5 °C, with a relative uncertainty of 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ lattice parameter∗ of Si (in vacuum, 22.5 ◦C) a 5.431 020 511(89)× 10−10 m 1.6× 10−8. The same NIST page notes that this is the lattice parameter (unit cell edge length) of an ideal single crystal of naturally occurring Si free of impurities and imperfections.\n\nA Wikipedia template that mirrors CODATA says its values were updated to the CODATA 2022 values and lists the same silicon value, 5.431020511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with a relative standard uncertainty of 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ lattice parameter of silicon | a = 5.431020511(89)×10−10 m | ur(a) = 1.6×10−8. So the value appears to be unchanged between the 2018 and 2022 adjustments, though I only confirmed the 2022 figure through a secondary source.\n\nFor background, NIST's published silicon lattice comparison papers, Kessler et al. (1994) and Hanke & Kessler (2005), appeared in the results, but I only saw their bibliographic records, not their contents."],"durationSeconds":5.7786662480000235,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NooAk5rAvVNDB1cPAGobEw","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RiKwLg51UaVDMkA3NeyAmG","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"experimental lattice constants Cu Al Ag Ge diamond table zero-point corrected PBE benchmark Haas Tran Blaha 2009\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Semiconductor Physics: A Density Functional Journey\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.13050\"},{\"title\":\"Assessing the Performance of Recent Density Functionals for Bulk Solids\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0903.4037\"},{\"title\":\"Performance of Tao-Mo semilocal density functional in projector-augmented-wave method\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1802.05185\"},{\"title\":\"Assessment of a nonempirical semilocal density functional on solids and surfaces\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1607.05252\"},{\"title\":\"Self-consistent Hessian-level meta-generalized gradient approximation\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.07046\"},{\"title\":\"Lattice dynamics and structural phase stability of group-IV elemental solids with the r$^2$SCAN functional\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.11177\"},{\"title\":\"calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal 47and4fs7z\",\"url\":\"https://typeset.io/pdf/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-47and4fs7z.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals\",\"url\":\"https://www.bohrium.com/paper-details/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals/811872347643445248-529\"},{\"title\":\"Dissemin is shutting down on January 1st, 2025\",\"url\":\"https://dissem.in/p/27732682/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals\"},{\"title\":\"dft pernot2015 jp509980w supp\",\"url\":\"https://iris.unito.it/retrieve/e27ce428-98c2-2581-e053-d805fe0acbaa/dft_pernot2015_jp509980w_supp.pdf\"}]\n\nI ran one search. It confirmed the Haas, Tran and Blaha 2009 paper and its method, but I couldn't retrieve its actual per-element numbers for Cu, Al, Ag and Ge.\n\n- **The paper:** Haas, Tran and Blaha, \"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals,\" Phys. Rev. B 79, 085104 (2009). A summary notes that experimental lattice constants were ZPAE-corrected, and statistical quantities like mean error and mean absolute error were used for result analysis.\n- **Al:** According to the same summary, for Al, most functionals except PBE yield accurate lattice constants.\n- **Ge:** A later study says PBE falsely predicts the diamond phases of Ge and Sn to be metals, whereas in reality Ge is a semiconductor. That error leads to strongly overestimated lattice constants in PBE.\n- **Cu, Al, Ag (uncorrected experiment vs. PBE):** A 2026 Hessian meta-GGA paper lists PBE values of 3.642 Å for Cu against 3.595 Å experimental, 4.042 Å for Al against 4.018 Å, and 4.147 Å for Ag against 4.062 Å (Table C.1, equilibrium lattice constants for the LC20 solids). These are not the ZPE-corrected Haas values, so treat them as a rough cross-check only.\n\nFor the exact ZPE-corrected Haas 2009 table, the original PRB article or its supporting information is needed.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"da0dc3b6-c5de-4e9f-8234-2ef78e8ae4a4","uuid":"30cd9de1-b8ed-4f1f-bd2a-6fb94444cb7a","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:27:47.216Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"experimental lattice constants Cu Al Ag Ge diamond table zero-point corrected PBE benchmark Haas Tran Blaha 2009","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01HY3zTJZbQF1WGBnPBntFZe","content":[{"title":"Semiconductor Physics: A Density Functional Journey","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.13050"},{"title":"Assessing the Performance of Recent Density Functionals for Bulk Solids","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0903.4037"},{"title":"Performance of Tao-Mo semilocal density functional in projector-augmented-wave method","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1802.05185"},{"title":"Assessment of a nonempirical semilocal density functional on solids and surfaces","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1607.05252"},{"title":"Self-consistent Hessian-level meta-generalized gradient approximation","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.07046"},{"title":"Lattice dynamics and structural phase stability of group-IV elemental solids with the r$^2$SCAN functional","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.11177"},{"title":"calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal 47and4fs7z","url":"https://typeset.io/pdf/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-47and4fs7z.pdf"},{"title":"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals","url":"https://www.bohrium.com/paper-details/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals/811872347643445248-529"},{"title":"Dissemin is shutting down on January 1st, 2025","url":"https://dissem.in/p/27732682/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals"},{"title":"dft pernot2015 jp509980w supp","url":"https://iris.unito.it/retrieve/e27ce428-98c2-2581-e053-d805fe0acbaa/dft_pernot2015_jp509980w_supp.pdf"}]},"I ran one search. It confirmed the Haas, Tran and Blaha 2009 paper and its method, but I couldn't retrieve its actual per-element numbers for Cu, Al, Ag and Ge.\n\n- **The paper:** Haas, Tran and Blaha, \"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals,\" Phys. Rev. B 79, 085104 (2009). A summary notes that experimental lattice constants were ZPAE-corrected, and statistical quantities like mean error and mean absolute error were used for result analysis.\n- **Al:** According to the same summary, for Al, most functionals except PBE yield accurate lattice constants.\n- **Ge:** A later study says PBE falsely predicts the diamond phases of Ge and Sn to be metals, whereas in reality Ge is a semiconductor. That error leads to strongly overestimated lattice constants in PBE.\n- **Cu, Al, Ag (uncorrected experiment vs. PBE):** A 2026 Hessian meta-GGA paper lists PBE values of 3.642 Å for Cu against 3.595 Å experimental, 4.042 Å for Al against 4.018 Å, and 4.147 Å for Ag against 4.062 Å (Table C.1, equilibrium lattice constants for the LC20 solids). These are not the ZPE-corrected Haas values, so treat them as a rough cross-check only.\n\nFor the exact ZPE-corrected Haas 2009 table, the original PRB article or its supporting information is needed."],"durationSeconds":7.065804897000024,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RiKwLg51UaVDMkA3NeyAmG","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
